{
  "verdict": "faithful_with_caveats",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Abstract propositional setting instead of first-order arithmetic with Gödel numbering: Reinhardt states Theorem 6 in a first-order theory of arithmetic extended with B, where q(x) is a formula with one free variable, [s] is a Gödel numeral, ng is a primitive recursive term representing syntactic negation, and axioms are universal closures of formulas. Target abstracts to an arbitrary type Fm with classical propositional connectives and arbitrary functions K, F : Fm → Fm, rendering q(ng[s]) directly as F (∼s) and eliding both arithmetic/quantifiers and the representation step ng[s] = [¬s] (a limitation declared by the author)."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Fixed point assumed as an explicit hypothesis rather than derived: in Reinhardt's Theorem 6, the existence of s with T ⊢ B(s ↔ Bq(ng[s])) is not a hypothesis of the theorem but is obtained in the proof from the arithmetical fixed-point (diagonal) theorem for any formula q(x). Target adds (∃ s, 𝓢 ⊢ K (s 🡘 K (F (∼s)))) as a hypothesis, weakening the statement relative to the full arithmetical theorem because the diagonal lemma cannot be proved in the abstract setting."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Abstract characterization of Con q: in the source, Con q is specifically defined as the quantified sentence ∀x¬(q(x) & q(ng(x))) ranging over all numbers. Target cannot express this definition and instead universally quantifies over an arbitrary sentence conF satisfying the sentence-level schema ∀ σ, 𝓑 ⊢ conF 🡒 ∼(F σ ⋏ F (∼σ)); this is an idealization that also slightly generalizes the theorem to any sentence with those instances."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Two-theory split 𝓑 ⪯ 𝓢: Reinhardt works in a single theory T whose axioms are optionally prefixed by B, whereas Target splits into a base theory 𝓑 (closed under necessitation and proving K-distribution, K φ 🡒 K (K φ), and the conF instances) and an extension 𝓢 (proving K (K φ 🡒 φ), the fixed point, and K (K σ 🡒 F σ), without requiring necessitation on 𝓢). This is a harmless strengthening (generalization), as taking 𝓑 = 𝓢 = T recovers the single-theory setting."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Omission of unused axioms from [P-R323]: Target omits the quantifier axiom B∀xP → ∀x Bp (unexpressible without quantifiers and unused in Theorem 6) as well as plain correctness Bp → p (in line with Reinhardt's remark in [P-R328] that only B(Bp → p) is used), and uses only classical propositional logic rather than full first-order logic and arithmetic."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Target faithfully captures the modal-epistemic structure and conclusion of Reinhardt's Theorem 6, including the exact forms of (B ≤ q), the fixed point, and the weakened reflection axiom B(Bp → p). Its caveats stem from the declared abstraction away from first-order arithmetic, quantifiers, and Gödel coding—most notably taking the diagonal fixed point as an explicit hypothesis rather than deriving it and characterizing Con q only through its sentence-level instances."
}
